00:00:05.223 --> 00:00:08.093
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:08.093 --> 00:00:10.003
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:10.003 --> 00:00:13.013
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:13.023 --> 00:00:14.703
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:14.703 --> 00:00:17.303
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:17.313 --> 00:00:19.323
things about the world at the same

00:00:19.323 --> 00:00:21.123
time as improving your English.

00:00:21.533 --> 00:00:23.943
I'm Alastair Budge, and this is the

00:00:23.973 --> 00:00:26.743
final part of our three-part mini-series

00:00:27.013 --> 00:00:29.273
on The Dark Side of Psychology.

00:00:29.753 --> 00:00:32.463
In part one, we looked at the Milgram

00:00:32.463 --> 00:00:34.623
experiment, and what it suggested about

00:00:34.633 --> 00:00:37.023
how ordinary people behave when an

00:00:37.023 --> 00:00:39.593
authority figure tells them to do harm.

00:00:40.183 --> 00:00:42.783
In part two, we turned to the work

00:00:42.823 --> 00:00:45.383
of Harry Harlow, the psychologist

00:00:45.613 --> 00:00:48.043
whose disturbing monkey experiments

00:00:48.283 --> 00:00:50.733
transformed our understanding of love.

00:00:51.033 --> 00:00:53.693
And today, in part three, we travel

00:00:53.713 --> 00:00:56.813
to early 1970s America for one of

00:00:56.813 --> 00:00:58.963
the most controversial studies in

00:00:58.963 --> 00:01:01.243
the history of psychiatry, both

00:01:01.513 --> 00:01:03.223
for what it revealed at the time,

00:01:03.483 --> 00:01:05.603
and for what a journalist revealed

00:01:05.603 --> 00:01:07.983
about it almost fifty years later.

00:01:08.513 --> 00:01:10.473
This is the story of

00:01:10.473 --> 00:01:12.313
The Rosenhan Experiment.

00:01:13.793 --> 00:01:16.783
"Thud" is the word we use in English

00:01:17.133 --> 00:01:19.743
to describe the sound a heavy object

00:01:19.743 --> 00:01:22.113
makes when it hits something else.

00:01:23.013 --> 00:01:24.843
It isn't a loud noise.

00:01:25.113 --> 00:01:28.283
It's different from a crash or a bang.

00:01:28.873 --> 00:01:32.613
It's a dull sound, the kind of sound a

00:01:32.633 --> 00:01:35.293
body might make if it hits the floor.

00:01:36.563 --> 00:01:41.103
And in 1969, a man in his late

00:01:41.113 --> 00:01:44.123
thirties walked up to the reception

00:01:44.123 --> 00:01:46.723
desk of a psychiatric hospital on

00:01:46.723 --> 00:01:48.873
the East Coast of the United States.

00:01:49.523 --> 00:01:51.903
He was clean and well-dressed.

00:01:52.323 --> 00:01:54.123
He seemed articulate.

00:01:54.983 --> 00:01:57.223
He gave his name as David

00:01:57.233 --> 00:01:59.383
Lurie, and explained that

00:01:59.383 --> 00:02:01.033
he had been hearing a voice.

00:02:01.993 --> 00:02:03.413
It says only one word,

00:02:03.813 --> 00:02:04.993
he told the receptionist.

00:02:05.983 --> 00:02:08.013
The word was "thud."

00:02:09.523 --> 00:02:12.213
Within hours, David Lurie was

00:02:12.253 --> 00:02:14.323
admitted to the hospital and

00:02:14.333 --> 00:02:16.623
diagnosed with schizophrenia.

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:19.570
He would soon realise that

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:22.200
getting in was the easy part.

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:26.790
See, David Lurie was not a real person.

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:29.430
The man's real name was

00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:32.290
David Rosenhan, and he was a

00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:34.390
Stanford psychology professor.

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:38.230
He was one of eight perfectly sane

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:43.002
people who, between 1969 and 1972,

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:46.110
would talk their way into psychiatric

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:48.330
hospitals across the United States,

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:51.170
in order to find out what happened

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:53.240
on the other side of the doors.

00:02:54.310 --> 00:02:57.200
Now, throughout history, societies

00:02:57.250 --> 00:02:59.310
across the world have dealt with

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:01.900
people suffering from mental illnesses

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:05.320
in different, often unkind, ways.

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:09.550
In the United States, by the mid-1950s,

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:12.120
there were around half a million

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:14.490
people in psychiatric hospitals.

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:16.640
Many had been there for

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:18.630
years, even decades.

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:21.830
Some had been committed by

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:23.970
families who could not cope.

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:27.340
Some had been committed by courts of law.

00:03:27.910 --> 00:03:30.460
Some had walked in voluntarily

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:33.230
and never been allowed to leave.

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:36.970
The diagnostic system that classified

00:03:36.970 --> 00:03:40.960
them was called the DSM-II, the

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:43.510
Diagnostic and Statistical Manual

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:46.090
of Mental Disorders, second edition.

00:03:47.750 --> 00:03:50.790
The idea behind it was to use a common

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:53.730
language to diagnose someone with a

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:56.130
mental disorder, so that it wasn't

00:03:56.140 --> 00:03:59.040
one psychiatrist's subjective opinion.

00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:04.250
To modern eyes, it is a strange document.

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:07.140
The categories were vague

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:09.020
and often overlapping.

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:12.460
A patient diagnosed by one psychiatrist

00:04:12.670 --> 00:04:15.330
as schizophrenic might be diagnosed

00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:18.350
by another as manic-depressive, or as

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:20.890
having a personality disorder, with no

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:23.780
clear way of resolving the disagreement.

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:28.330
And most famously, homosexuality was

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:31.170
still listed as a mental disorder.

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:34.860
The culture, meanwhile, had

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:37.510
begun to question the assumed

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:39.960
understanding of mental disorders.

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:45.690
In 1962, Ken Kesey had published One

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:47.740
Flew Over the Cuckoo's Nest, which

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:51.090
became an Oscar-winning film in 1975.

00:04:51.950 --> 00:04:55.580
The Scottish psychiatrist R. D. Laing had

00:04:55.580 --> 00:04:58.160
argued that what we called schizophrenia

00:04:58.470 --> 00:05:01.120
was often a rational response to

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:03.550
an intolerable family situation.

00:05:04.010 --> 00:05:06.140
The French philosopher Michel Foucault

00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:09.000
had published Madness and Civilisation,

00:05:09.370 --> 00:05:12.190
which was his history of how Western

00:05:12.190 --> 00:05:14.840
societies had used the diagnosis of

00:05:14.850 --> 00:05:17.890
madness as a tool of social control.

00:05:18.710 --> 00:05:20.880
In other words, people were getting

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:23.910
more and more interested in the science

00:05:23.970 --> 00:05:26.860
behind mental illness, and more and more

00:05:26.890 --> 00:05:29.480
critical of the understanding of the day.

00:05:30.610 --> 00:05:33.210
And David Rosenhan was one of those

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:36.180
people, someone who set out to conduct

00:05:36.230 --> 00:05:38.790
an experiment into mental illness.

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:41.910
And specifically, whether psychiatric

00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:43.900
professionals at the time could

00:05:43.910 --> 00:05:46.530
distinguish between someone who was

00:05:46.540 --> 00:05:49.500
mentally ill and someone who was not.

00:05:50.890 --> 00:05:53.060
Rosenhan was a professor at

00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:55.990
Stanford, and an admired teacher.

00:05:56.560 --> 00:05:58.870
He was also, by all accounts, a

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:01.220
charismatic and persuasive figure,

00:06:01.610 --> 00:06:03.640
the kind of man who could get other

00:06:03.640 --> 00:06:06.160
people to do unusual things for him.

00:06:07.434 --> 00:06:10.464
His experiment would go as follows.

00:06:10.994 --> 00:06:13.354
He would recruit a small group of

00:06:13.354 --> 00:06:16.134
so-called pseudopatients, people who

00:06:16.134 --> 00:06:18.904
were perfectly healthy but would present

00:06:18.904 --> 00:06:21.394
themselves at psychiatric hospitals.

00:06:22.524 --> 00:06:24.334
When they arrived, they

00:06:24.334 --> 00:06:26.724
would claim to hear voices.

00:06:27.894 --> 00:06:31.334
The voices, they would say, were unclear,

00:06:31.624 --> 00:06:34.074
but they could make out a single word.

00:06:34.784 --> 00:06:38.924
Either "thud," or "empty," or

00:06:39.134 --> 00:06:40.974
"hollow." That would be the

00:06:41.044 --> 00:06:42.884
only symptom they reported.

00:06:43.774 --> 00:06:46.294
In every other respect, their name,

00:06:46.294 --> 00:06:48.024
their occupation, their life's

00:06:48.024 --> 00:06:50.124
story, they would tell the truth.

00:06:51.784 --> 00:06:53.804
Eight pseudopatients took part.

00:06:54.444 --> 00:06:56.714
There was a psychology graduate student

00:06:56.724 --> 00:06:59.324
in his twenties, three psychologists,

00:06:59.514 --> 00:07:01.444
one of whom was Rosenhan himself,

00:07:01.704 --> 00:07:04.084
a paediatrician, a psychiatrist,

00:07:04.324 --> 00:07:06.434
a painter, and a housewife.

00:07:07.064 --> 00:07:09.604
Five men and three women.

00:07:10.864 --> 00:07:13.274
They presented themselves at twelve

00:07:13.284 --> 00:07:16.434
hospitals in five different states, on

00:07:16.434 --> 00:07:19.604
the East Coast and the West Coast, in old

00:07:19.634 --> 00:07:22.714
grand institutions and in shiny new ones.

00:07:23.234 --> 00:07:24.904
The idea was to get a

00:07:24.904 --> 00:07:26.734
representative cross-section.

00:07:28.054 --> 00:07:29.904
All eight were admitted.

00:07:30.684 --> 00:07:33.094
All but one were diagnosed

00:07:33.114 --> 00:07:34.154
with schizophrenia.

00:07:34.804 --> 00:07:36.944
The exception, at an expensive

00:07:36.974 --> 00:07:39.164
private hospital, was diagnosed

00:07:39.164 --> 00:07:41.144
with manic-depressive psychosis,

00:07:41.494 --> 00:07:42.624
which is the term for what

00:07:42.703 --> 00:07:45.044
we now call bipolar disorder.

00:07:46.934 --> 00:07:49.844
Once admitted, the rules were simple.

00:07:50.594 --> 00:07:53.554
They were to behave entirely normally.

00:07:54.064 --> 00:07:55.444
They were to drop the

00:07:55.444 --> 00:07:56.724
symptoms immediately.

00:07:57.254 --> 00:07:58.604
They were to be honest about

00:07:58.714 --> 00:08:00.974
everything, except the fact they

00:08:00.974 --> 00:08:02.414
had invented the voices in order

00:08:02.414 --> 00:08:03.584
to try to get into the hospital.

00:08:04.862 --> 00:08:06.862
The first real eye-opener

00:08:07.102 --> 00:08:09.482
for these pseudopatients was

00:08:09.522 --> 00:08:11.242
the hospitals themselves.

00:08:11.952 --> 00:08:14.042
They were strange places.

00:08:14.582 --> 00:08:16.552
The wards were enormously

00:08:16.612 --> 00:08:19.442
understaffed, with sometimes as few

00:08:19.462 --> 00:08:22.272
as one nurse for fifty patients.

00:08:23.302 --> 00:08:25.272
The staff rarely interacted

00:08:25.282 --> 00:08:26.652
with the patients beyond

00:08:26.652 --> 00:08:28.932
mealtimes and medication rounds.

00:08:29.622 --> 00:08:32.612
When they did interact, they often spoke

00:08:32.852 --> 00:08:35.172
as if the patients were not really there.

00:08:36.182 --> 00:08:38.852
One pseudopatient noted that a female

00:08:38.852 --> 00:08:41.662
nurse adjusted her bra in front of

00:08:41.662 --> 00:08:44.292
a day room full of male patients,

00:08:44.472 --> 00:08:46.672
with no apparent self-consciousness,

00:08:46.962 --> 00:08:48.672
as if they were furniture.

00:08:50.222 --> 00:08:52.542
Another noted that staff would walk

00:08:52.542 --> 00:08:54.412
straight past patients trying to talk

00:08:54.412 --> 00:08:57.042
to them, eyes fixed on the corridor

00:08:57.042 --> 00:08:59.362
ahead, pretending not to hear.

00:09:01.062 --> 00:09:03.512
The pseudopatients took continuous

00:09:03.512 --> 00:09:05.232
notes on what they observed.

00:09:05.642 --> 00:09:08.072
And they took these notes openly,

00:09:08.442 --> 00:09:10.472
sitting in the communal rooms with

00:09:10.472 --> 00:09:12.552
notebooks on their laps, talking

00:09:12.762 --> 00:09:14.352
to other patients and staff.

00:09:15.122 --> 00:09:17.531
None of the staff ever

00:09:17.852 --> 00:09:19.022
questioned this behaviour.

00:09:19.732 --> 00:09:22.322
But it did go into the patients' files.

00:09:22.982 --> 00:09:26.612
One nurse's note read, simply: "patient

00:09:26.642 --> 00:09:28.962
engages in writing behaviour", as

00:09:28.962 --> 00:09:31.172
if the act of writing had become

00:09:31.172 --> 00:09:33.412
a symptom of a mental disorder.

00:09:34.705 --> 00:09:37.265
Every interaction with staff was

00:09:37.265 --> 00:09:39.705
filtered through the diagnosis.

00:09:40.355 --> 00:09:42.775
A pseudopatient walking up and down the

00:09:42.775 --> 00:09:45.285
corridor was, according to one note,

00:09:45.875 --> 00:09:48.555
"pacing nervously." A pseudopatient

00:09:48.835 --> 00:09:51.235
who said he was bored was demonstrating

00:09:51.715 --> 00:09:54.215
"flat affect", which is when someone

00:09:54.215 --> 00:09:56.235
is unable to show outward emotion.

00:09:57.525 --> 00:09:59.225
And they found that getting

00:09:59.225 --> 00:10:01.395
themselves discharged was

00:10:01.405 --> 00:10:03.645
far harder than anticipated.

00:10:05.045 --> 00:10:07.235
A pseudopatient who asked when

00:10:07.235 --> 00:10:09.295
he could be discharged was being

00:10:09.815 --> 00:10:12.945
"uncooperative" or "denying his illness."

00:10:14.145 --> 00:10:16.675
Interestingly enough, the pseudopatients

00:10:16.675 --> 00:10:19.235
reported that the actual patients on the

00:10:19.235 --> 00:10:22.895
ward, the people with real mental illness,

00:10:23.255 --> 00:10:25.665
often saw through them immediately.

00:10:26.505 --> 00:10:28.845
"You're not crazy," one patient said

00:10:28.845 --> 00:10:30.535
to a pseudopatient on the third day.

00:10:30.805 --> 00:10:32.815
"You're a journalist, or a professor.

00:10:33.095 --> 00:10:34.685
You're checking up on the hospital."

00:10:36.165 --> 00:10:38.995
Of course, the patient, the one who

00:10:38.995 --> 00:10:41.485
was in the mental hospital, was right.

00:10:43.085 --> 00:10:45.385
Now, the pseudopatients did

00:10:45.385 --> 00:10:47.355
manage to get out eventually.

00:10:47.905 --> 00:10:50.025
They were detained for an average

00:10:50.065 --> 00:10:51.945
of nineteen days, with the

00:10:51.955 --> 00:10:54.485
longest stay being fifty-two days.

00:10:55.485 --> 00:10:57.735
They were given, between them, more

00:10:57.735 --> 00:11:00.745
than two thousand pills, which they

00:11:00.745 --> 00:11:02.385
secretly flushed down the toilet.

00:11:03.705 --> 00:11:05.595
And when they were eventually

00:11:05.595 --> 00:11:08.415
discharged, every single one was

00:11:08.415 --> 00:11:10.935
released with the same diagnosis:

00:11:11.675 --> 00:11:13.925
"schizophrenia in remission."

00:11:15.075 --> 00:11:17.355
They hadn't been cured, and there

00:11:17.355 --> 00:11:19.545
was no suggestion that the initial

00:11:19.545 --> 00:11:21.385
diagnosis had been incorrect.

00:11:21.735 --> 00:11:23.525
They were just temporarily

00:11:23.854 --> 00:11:25.160
not showing symptoms.

00:11:27.294 --> 00:11:30.195
The implication was devastating: the

00:11:30.195 --> 00:11:33.015
psychiatrists at twelve different

00:11:33.025 --> 00:11:36.075
hospitals had been unable to tell

00:11:36.105 --> 00:11:38.645
sane people from insane ones.

00:11:39.545 --> 00:11:41.565
Once they had been admitted, the

00:11:41.565 --> 00:11:43.685
pseudopatients could not get themselves

00:11:43.685 --> 00:11:46.995
out by behaving sanely, because every act

00:11:46.995 --> 00:11:50.405
of sanity was interpreted as a symptom of

00:11:50.405 --> 00:11:52.575
the illness they had been labelled with.

00:11:53.881 --> 00:11:57.361
It seemed like damning evidence that the

00:11:57.371 --> 00:12:00.091
prevailing diagnostic system was broken.

00:12:01.554 --> 00:12:05.454
In 1973, Rosenhan published his findings

00:12:05.474 --> 00:12:07.714
in the prestigious journal Science.

00:12:08.394 --> 00:12:10.404
The paper was titled "On

00:12:10.504 --> 00:12:13.314
Being Sane in Insane Places."

00:12:14.344 --> 00:12:15.904
And as you might imagine,

00:12:16.154 --> 00:12:17.834
it was a bombshell.

00:12:19.034 --> 00:12:20.964
After the publication, a

00:12:20.964 --> 00:12:22.374
well-known research and

00:12:22.374 --> 00:12:24.234
teaching hospital got in touch.

00:12:24.714 --> 00:12:26.804
Its staff had heard about Rosenhan's

00:12:26.914 --> 00:12:29.524
findings, and they were unimpressed.

00:12:29.924 --> 00:12:31.704
Nothing like that, they said,

00:12:31.914 --> 00:12:33.524
could happen at their institution.

00:12:34.514 --> 00:12:37.044
So Rosenhan proposed a test.

00:12:37.844 --> 00:12:39.944
Over the next three months, he

00:12:39.944 --> 00:12:41.994
would send pseudopatients to them.

00:12:42.864 --> 00:12:45.284
The staff were told to expect them,

00:12:45.574 --> 00:12:48.304
and every single person who came

00:12:48.324 --> 00:12:50.384
through the doors would be rated on

00:12:50.384 --> 00:12:53.684
a scale of one to ten, according to

00:12:53.864 --> 00:12:56.024
how likely they were to be a fake.

00:12:57.494 --> 00:12:59.234
Over those three months,

00:12:59.558 --> 00:13:02.344
193 patients were admitted.

00:13:03.214 --> 00:13:05.094
Forty-one of them, more than

00:13:05.104 --> 00:13:08.034
one in five, were confidently

00:13:08.054 --> 00:13:10.904
identified as pseudopatients by

00:13:10.904 --> 00:13:12.814
at least one member of staff.

00:13:13.644 --> 00:13:16.674
Nineteen were suspected by a psychiatrist

00:13:16.974 --> 00:13:18.874
and a second member of staff.

00:13:20.304 --> 00:13:23.334
Then Rosenhan revealed that he had

00:13:23.334 --> 00:13:26.479
not sent a single pseudopatient.

00:13:27.414 --> 00:13:30.124
The reputation of American psychiatry

00:13:30.334 --> 00:13:32.074
took yet another battering.

00:13:32.634 --> 00:13:35.114
The paper was discussed in newspapers,

00:13:35.144 --> 00:13:38.044
on television and in medical journals.

00:13:38.714 --> 00:13:40.894
The American Psychiatric Association

00:13:41.144 --> 00:13:43.194
responded furiously, with

00:13:43.214 --> 00:13:45.234
established figures denouncing

00:13:45.234 --> 00:13:47.504
the study as methodologically

00:13:47.514 --> 00:13:49.994
flawed and morally questionable.

00:13:51.114 --> 00:13:53.194
But the damage was done.

00:13:54.054 --> 00:13:57.774
In 1980, the DSM, the framework

00:13:57.774 --> 00:13:59.694
and criteria for diagnosing

00:13:59.694 --> 00:14:01.364
mental illness, was updated

00:14:01.404 --> 00:14:04.304
again in the form of the DSM-III.

00:14:05.194 --> 00:14:07.194
The objective was to make sure that

00:14:07.254 --> 00:14:09.734
two psychiatrists examining the same

00:14:09.734 --> 00:14:12.234
patient would reach the same conclusion.

00:14:13.541 --> 00:14:15.724
The document came to four times the

00:14:15.724 --> 00:14:18.924
length of the DSM-II, its predecessor,

00:14:19.224 --> 00:14:22.044
and had specific, checklist-style

00:14:22.044 --> 00:14:24.560
criteria for every diagnosis.

00:14:25.370 --> 00:14:27.040
It wasn't perfect, of course,

00:14:27.410 --> 00:14:29.690
but it was a step forward.

00:14:30.860 --> 00:14:33.340
Now, this was part of a sweeping

00:14:33.340 --> 00:14:35.800
movement to change the treatment of

00:14:35.800 --> 00:14:37.930
people suffering from mental illness, a

00:14:37.930 --> 00:14:40.676
movement that had begun before Rosenhan.

00:14:42.020 --> 00:14:44.580
Specifically, there was a shift away

00:14:44.590 --> 00:14:46.910
from large state mental hospitals

00:14:47.170 --> 00:14:49.400
in favour of community-based care.

00:14:50.390 --> 00:14:52.660
Over the next two decades, hundreds

00:14:52.680 --> 00:14:54.550
of thousands of patients would be

00:14:54.550 --> 00:14:57.180
moved out of long-stay institutions.

00:14:58.650 --> 00:15:01.810
And as for Rosenhan, his study was

00:15:01.810 --> 00:15:04.030
taught as one of the foundational

00:15:04.040 --> 00:15:06.990
studies of social psychology to explain

00:15:06.990 --> 00:15:09.340
why the old asylum system had failed.

00:15:09.750 --> 00:15:11.690
It became part of the canon.

00:15:11.810 --> 00:15:14.180
It was cited thousands of times.

00:15:14.580 --> 00:15:16.959
The story of the eight pseudopatients

00:15:17.080 --> 00:15:19.780
was told and retold in textbooks

00:15:19.780 --> 00:15:22.280
and undergraduate lectures, and

00:15:22.580 --> 00:15:25.960
when Rosenhan died in 2012, he

00:15:25.960 --> 00:15:29.180
died a hero of modern psychiatry.

00:15:30.350 --> 00:15:33.320
But just a few years later came

00:15:33.380 --> 00:15:36.500
the twist: the study itself might

00:15:36.500 --> 00:15:38.930
not have happened, at least not

00:15:38.960 --> 00:15:41.200
in the way Rosenhan said it did.

00:15:42.110 --> 00:15:44.600
And this is thanks to the work of

00:15:44.600 --> 00:15:46.590
a woman called Susannah Cahalan.

00:15:47.230 --> 00:15:49.560
Now, Susannah Cahalan had no

00:15:49.561 --> 00:15:51.480
intention of "outing" Rosenhan.

00:15:52.150 --> 00:15:54.860
She had, herself, been the subject

00:15:54.870 --> 00:15:56.810
of a serious diagnostic failure.

00:15:57.720 --> 00:15:59.730
She was twenty-four years old at

00:15:59.750 --> 00:16:02.190
the time, and a successful young

00:16:02.190 --> 00:16:04.320
journalist at the New York Post.

00:16:05.280 --> 00:16:08.360
One day she started to feel strange.

00:16:09.040 --> 00:16:10.900
She became paranoid that her

00:16:10.920 --> 00:16:12.639
boyfriend was cheating on her.

00:16:12.910 --> 00:16:14.370
She became convinced that

00:16:14.380 --> 00:16:16.180
the people on television were

00:16:16.180 --> 00:16:18.030
sending her personal messages.

00:16:18.890 --> 00:16:21.260
Then came the seizures.

00:16:22.060 --> 00:16:23.990
She was sent to a psychiatrist who

00:16:23.990 --> 00:16:26.620
diagnosed her with bipolar disorder.

00:16:27.420 --> 00:16:29.500
Then with a psychotic episode.

00:16:29.710 --> 00:16:31.420
Then with schizophrenia.

00:16:32.000 --> 00:16:34.400
The doctors prepared paperwork to

00:16:34.400 --> 00:16:36.910
commit her to a psychiatric hospital.

00:16:38.099 --> 00:16:41.150
Her parents, in desperation, called

00:16:41.160 --> 00:16:44.220
in one more specialist, a neurologist.

00:16:45.430 --> 00:16:47.710
This neurologist asked her to

00:16:47.710 --> 00:16:50.370
draw a clock on a piece of paper.

00:16:51.080 --> 00:16:54.040
She drew all twelve numbers crammed

00:16:54.070 --> 00:16:56.070
onto the right side of the circle,

00:16:56.460 --> 00:16:59.310
leaving the left side completely blank.

00:17:00.520 --> 00:17:02.990
It was a classic sign of injury

00:17:03.170 --> 00:17:04.820
to one side of the brain.

00:17:05.610 --> 00:17:08.090
The autoimmune disease he then

00:17:08.090 --> 00:17:10.600
diagnosed was a condition that had

00:17:10.600 --> 00:17:12.360
only been identified in the medical

00:17:12.360 --> 00:17:14.530
literature a couple of years earlier.

00:17:15.550 --> 00:17:16.780
She got the right treatment,

00:17:16.950 --> 00:17:18.580
and she recovered fully.

00:17:19.060 --> 00:17:21.200
But without this diagnosis and

00:17:21.200 --> 00:17:22.670
this treatment, she would have

00:17:22.670 --> 00:17:24.670
been labelled as schizophrenic.

00:17:25.610 --> 00:17:27.800
The book she wrote about the experience,

00:17:28.080 --> 00:17:30.670
Brain on Fire, became a bestseller.

00:17:31.860 --> 00:17:34.000
She then intended to write a book about

00:17:34.000 --> 00:17:37.380
Rosenhan that celebrated him as a hero

00:17:37.570 --> 00:17:40.310
of American psychiatry, someone who had

00:17:40.310 --> 00:17:43.180
done invaluable work on how psychiatric

00:17:43.180 --> 00:17:46.390
patients are identified and diagnosed.

00:17:47.060 --> 00:17:49.910
So she set out to find the eight

00:17:50.099 --> 00:17:52.580
pseudopatients from the original study.

00:17:53.580 --> 00:17:56.090
But no matter how hard she

00:17:56.090 --> 00:17:58.340
looked, she could only find two

00:17:58.600 --> 00:18:00.387
of the original pseudopatients.

00:18:01.280 --> 00:18:03.920
Over years of investigation, working

00:18:04.150 --> 00:18:06.510
from Rosenhan's own papers, his

00:18:06.690 --> 00:18:08.940
correspondence, his notes, and the

00:18:08.960 --> 00:18:11.340
surviving medical records, she was able

00:18:11.340 --> 00:18:14.550
to confirm only Rosenhan himself and a

00:18:14.550 --> 00:18:16.950
graduate student named Bill Underwood.

00:18:18.280 --> 00:18:19.430
The remaining six

00:18:19.450 --> 00:18:21.660
pseudopatients were ghosts.

00:18:22.140 --> 00:18:23.420
There were references

00:18:23.420 --> 00:18:25.100
in the paper to a "Mrs.

00:18:25.379 --> 00:18:28.426
M." and a "Mr F"  but no surviving

00:18:28.426 --> 00:18:30.496
record of who they actually were,

00:18:30.846 --> 00:18:32.776
or which hospitals they went to.

00:18:33.586 --> 00:18:34.896
Cahalan kept digging.

00:18:35.366 --> 00:18:37.486
She found Rosenhan's own admission

00:18:37.486 --> 00:18:40.306
record from Haverford State Hospital, the

00:18:40.526 --> 00:18:42.736
institution he had checked himself into.

00:18:43.336 --> 00:18:44.776
He had been admitted there.

00:18:45.026 --> 00:18:46.766
That was not up for debate.

00:18:47.156 --> 00:18:49.486
But the hospital records did

00:18:49.486 --> 00:18:51.696
not match his published account.

00:18:52.946 --> 00:18:54.856
According to Rosenhan, he had

00:18:54.856 --> 00:18:58.066
reported only the single word "thud."

00:18:59.066 --> 00:19:01.066
According to the actual medical

00:19:01.066 --> 00:19:03.306
chart, he had reported much

00:19:03.316 --> 00:19:04.766
more disturbing symptoms.

00:19:05.636 --> 00:19:07.796
He had said he could hear what

00:19:07.836 --> 00:19:08.926
other people were thinking.

00:19:09.296 --> 00:19:10.946
He had said he was sensitive

00:19:10.946 --> 00:19:13.256
to radio waves, and that he had

00:19:13.266 --> 00:19:15.336
tried to block the voices out by

00:19:15.336 --> 00:19:17.436
putting copper pots over his ears.

00:19:17.966 --> 00:19:19.136
He had said he had been

00:19:19.136 --> 00:19:20.806
having suicidal thoughts.

00:19:21.156 --> 00:19:23.216
And he had said all of this

00:19:23.646 --> 00:19:25.236
had been going on for months.

00:19:26.216 --> 00:19:28.266
These were not the symptoms of

00:19:28.266 --> 00:19:30.766
a man being unjustly admitted

00:19:30.986 --> 00:19:33.376
because of one ambiguous word.

00:19:33.936 --> 00:19:35.766
They were classic markers of

00:19:35.806 --> 00:19:38.096
severe psychiatric illness.

00:19:39.566 --> 00:19:41.986
Cahalan found Bill Underwood,

00:19:42.026 --> 00:19:43.566
the one other confirmed

00:19:43.566 --> 00:19:45.816
pseudopatient, and interviewed him.

00:19:46.516 --> 00:19:48.716
He was by then in his seventies.

00:19:49.366 --> 00:19:51.346
He remembered the experience vividly.

00:19:51.816 --> 00:19:53.866
The food, he said, was terrible.

00:19:54.046 --> 00:19:55.836
The boredom was overwhelming.

00:19:56.426 --> 00:19:58.016
He had written poems on

00:19:58.046 --> 00:19:59.906
toilet paper to pass the time.

00:20:00.476 --> 00:20:02.296
He had eventually got out only

00:20:02.296 --> 00:20:03.956
when his wife had come in with a

00:20:03.966 --> 00:20:05.916
lawyer, threatening legal action.

00:20:06.906 --> 00:20:08.476
The hospital had then released

00:20:08.476 --> 00:20:09.896
him, with the diagnosis

00:20:10.126 --> 00:20:11.656
"schizophrenia in remission."

00:20:12.196 --> 00:20:14.566
His account broadly matched what

00:20:14.566 --> 00:20:16.456
Rosenhan had described in the paper.

00:20:16.996 --> 00:20:19.226
But he was just one person.

00:20:20.432 --> 00:20:23.486
Cahalan, in fact, found a ninth person,

00:20:23.726 --> 00:20:25.886
someone who was sent to a hospital,

00:20:25.966 --> 00:20:28.126
but wasn't included in Rosenhan's

00:20:28.156 --> 00:20:30.466
paper because his experience of the

00:20:30.466 --> 00:20:33.082
hospital was actually very positive.

00:20:33.852 --> 00:20:36.332
It seemed that he'd been excluded

00:20:36.632 --> 00:20:38.882
because his case simply didn't match

00:20:38.902 --> 00:20:41.132
what Rosenhan had intended to show.

00:20:42.142 --> 00:20:44.412
Then there was the follow-up study,

00:20:44.552 --> 00:20:47.222
the one with the 41 false positives.

00:20:47.982 --> 00:20:49.592
Cahalan went looking for the

00:20:49.592 --> 00:20:51.902
hospital that had supposedly run it.

00:20:52.562 --> 00:20:53.972
She found references to

00:20:53.972 --> 00:20:56.022
it in Rosenhan's archives.

00:20:56.332 --> 00:20:58.712
But she could find no evidence

00:20:58.992 --> 00:21:00.562
that it had actually happened.

00:21:01.162 --> 00:21:03.312
The records did not exist.

00:21:03.672 --> 00:21:05.202
The staff who would have been

00:21:05.202 --> 00:21:07.242
involved had no memory of it.

00:21:07.942 --> 00:21:09.312
The newspaper coverage from the

00:21:09.312 --> 00:21:11.822
time, which would surely have noticed

00:21:11.832 --> 00:21:13.802
such a study, did not mention it.

00:21:14.938 --> 00:21:16.906
She also found something else.

00:21:17.631 --> 00:21:19.856
Rosenhan had become a celebrated

00:21:19.856 --> 00:21:21.626
figure on the back of the paper.

00:21:22.316 --> 00:21:24.036
He had been contracted to write

00:21:24.066 --> 00:21:25.656
a full book about the study,

00:21:25.866 --> 00:21:28.856
and paid a substantial advance.

00:21:29.306 --> 00:21:31.266
He had spent years promising

00:21:31.266 --> 00:21:32.566
to deliver the manuscript.

00:21:32.956 --> 00:21:34.736
But he'd never finished it.

00:21:35.556 --> 00:21:37.826
The publisher even sued him

00:21:38.136 --> 00:21:39.946
for the return of the advance.

00:21:40.556 --> 00:21:42.906
He died without ever explaining

00:21:43.066 --> 00:21:44.492
the eight pseudopatients.

00:21:45.626 --> 00:21:48.096
Cahalan's suspicion, which she hints

00:21:48.096 --> 00:21:50.786
at in her book, is that he could not.

00:21:52.496 --> 00:21:54.836
In the end, Cahalan concluded that

00:21:54.836 --> 00:21:57.446
Rosenhan had probably fabricated

00:21:57.446 --> 00:21:59.406
or substantially embellished

00:21:59.456 --> 00:22:01.036
much of his famous paper.

00:22:01.616 --> 00:22:03.146
Perhaps not all of it.

00:22:03.596 --> 00:22:05.166
The study had clearly involved at

00:22:05.166 --> 00:22:07.736
least two people, perhaps a few more.

00:22:08.286 --> 00:22:10.976
But the dramatic, twelve-hospital,

00:22:11.286 --> 00:22:13.026
eight-pseudopatient experiment

00:22:13.236 --> 00:22:15.816
that had reshaped psychiatry may

00:22:15.836 --> 00:22:17.466
never have happened, at least

00:22:17.686 --> 00:22:20.006
not as Rosenhan had described it.

00:22:21.046 --> 00:22:23.476
So where does this leave things,

00:22:23.566 --> 00:22:25.656
and what is the prevailing wisdom

00:22:25.996 --> 00:22:28.226
on Rosenhan and this experiment?

00:22:28.926 --> 00:22:31.096
Well, the Rosenhan study has

00:22:31.096 --> 00:22:32.896
not been fully discredited.

00:22:33.275 --> 00:22:35.966
It's possible some version of it happened.

00:22:36.546 --> 00:22:39.016
The questions it raised about diagnostic

00:22:39.046 --> 00:22:41.336
reliability were good questions.

00:22:41.566 --> 00:22:44.336
The reforms it helped produce, including

00:22:44.336 --> 00:22:46.666
the move to more specific diagnostic

00:22:46.666 --> 00:22:49.686
criteria, were arguably for the better.

00:22:50.526 --> 00:22:53.176
But the iconic version of the story,

00:22:53.556 --> 00:22:55.646
the one that was taught in every

00:22:55.656 --> 00:22:57.916
introductory psychology textbook for

00:22:57.916 --> 00:23:00.506
nearly half a century, was probably

00:23:00.876 --> 00:23:03.976
somewhere between "exaggerated" and,

00:23:04.216 --> 00:23:07.106
as one journal put it, "a spectacularly

00:23:07.106 --> 00:23:09.896
successful case of scientific fraud".

00:23:11.836 --> 00:23:14.026
OK, then, that is it for this

00:23:14.066 --> 00:23:16.096
episode, and with this comes an

00:23:16.096 --> 00:23:17.976
end to our three-part mini-series

00:23:18.226 --> 00:23:20.086
on The Dark Side of Psychology.

00:23:20.366 --> 00:23:21.836
I hope you enjoyed them, and

00:23:21.836 --> 00:23:23.316
that they shone a light on some

00:23:23.356 --> 00:23:25.246
unusual experiments into what

00:23:25.246 --> 00:23:28.196
makes us obey, love, and believe.

00:23:28.956 --> 00:23:30.736
You've been listening to English Learning

00:23:30.736 --> 00:23:33.396
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:23:33.736 --> 00:23:36.626
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:23:36.636 --> 00:23:39.066
I'll catch you in the next episode.

